WEBVTT

00:00:05.117 --> 00:00:09.077
You may be watching this film because you're just starting to self-catheterize.

00:00:09.077 --> 00:00:11.927
When you’re having problems emptying your bladder completely,

00:00:11.927 --> 00:00:15.927
intermittent catheterization is often considered the best option.

00:00:15.927 --> 00:00:20.127
Many people self-catheterize every day, many more than you might think.

00:00:20.127 --> 00:00:25.017
You're definitely not alone.

00:00:25.017 --> 00:00:33.617
Intermittent catheterization or IC involves emptying your bladder regularly with a hollow tube, a catheter.

00:00:33.617 --> 00:00:37.907
The catheter is inserted in your urethra, which is the tube between the bladder and the exit,

00:00:37.907 --> 00:00:43.287
and into your bladder, allowing the urine to flow down the catheter and out.

00:00:43.287 --> 00:00:50.867
It may feel like a strange sensation to catheterize, but it shouldn’t be painful to insert or remove.

00:00:50.867 --> 00:00:54.207
There are many benefits of IC, as by emptying your bladder completely,

00:00:54.207 --> 00:01:02.207
and done regularly it will help prevent UTIs, and reduce kidney damage, maintaining good bladder health.

00:01:07.717 --> 00:01:12.127
Other positive factors may be that it reduces urine leakage, frequent urges to urinate

00:01:12.127 --> 00:01:20.127
and the need to get up at night to go to the toilet, whilst having the freedom to maintain your sex life if you wanted to.

00:01:26.167 --> 00:01:29.367
It’s also important you learn a good technique and use the right equipment.

00:01:29.367 --> 00:01:35.917
Both will help you avoid infections or other complications. Our health depends on emptying our bladder regularly.

00:01:35.917 --> 00:01:40.017
It's one of the ways that the body gets rid of waste.

00:01:40.017 --> 00:01:46.167
Many adults and children do IC regularly, even those who may have limited mobility or coordination issues.

00:01:46.167 --> 00:01:50.367
It is quite usual to feel a little anxious or worried when starting something new for the first time,

00:01:50.367 --> 00:01:55.967
but with practice and support you will soon become more confident with intermittent catheterization.

00:01:55.967 --> 00:01:59.267
It will soon become a natural part of your daily routine.

00:01:59.267 --> 00:02:02.117
It can be done anywhere you would normally want to empty your bladder,

00:02:02.117 --> 00:02:09.517
which means you can travel or work or just get on with your life, with one less thing to worry about.

00:02:09.517 --> 00:02:14.117
The number of times you catheterize will vary individually and also depend upon fluid intake.

00:02:14.117 --> 00:02:17.867
Your clinician will advise you on the right regime for you.

00:02:17.867 --> 00:02:22.217
Selecting a suitable catheter, based on informed knowledge of what is available, is important

00:02:22.217 --> 00:02:29.017
as is choosing a catheter that is right for you, as not all catheters are suitable for everyone.

00:02:29.017 --> 00:02:32.867
Taking the long-term perspective is as important as getting a good start.

00:02:32.867 --> 00:02:35.217
The surface of the catheter is important.

00:02:35.217 --> 00:02:40.317
The surface of the catheter is in direct contact with the urethra during each catheterization.

00:02:40.317 --> 00:02:47.017
A smooth, well lubricated, hydrophilic-coated surface can make a difference, especially when used for a long time.

00:02:47.017 --> 00:02:52.417
LoFric is a single-use hydrophilic-coated catheter, adapted to the natural conditions of the urethra

00:02:52.417 --> 00:02:59.217
that minimizes complications of catheterization, even after long term use.

00:02:59.217 --> 00:03:05.017
This is just a guide for you when having to consider doing IC and for information purposes only.

00:03:05.017 --> 00:03:18.381
Please always seek advice from your clinician first.
